{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/master/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Threagile",
          "informationUri": "https://threagile.io",
          "version": "1.0.0",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "internet-reachable-datastore",
              "name": "Stage VII - Internet-Reachable Datastore With No Mitigation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PASTA Stage VII evaluates residual risk. A datastore (database, cache, object store) that can be reached from the internet, either directly or via a chain of HTTP/plain protocol hops, with no network segmentation represents one of the highest-risk configurations in any architecture. Attackers who compromise any internet-facing component gain a direct path to sensitive stored data.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Mass data exfiltration of all stored records, ransomware encryption of datastores, complete loss of data integrity, and regulatory breach notification obligations.\n\n\nMitigation: Place all datastores in a dedicated backend trust boundary with no direct internet access. Ensure all communication links between internet-facing assets and datastores go through the application tier (no direct datastore exposure). Apply defense-in-depth with connection-level authentication and network ACLs.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Are all datastores behind at least one application-tier trust boundary?"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Database_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html",
              "properties": {
                "cwe": "CWE-284",
                "function": "architecture",
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "stride": "information-disclosure"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "missing-threat-scenario",
              "name": "Stage I - No Threat Scenarios Defined for Internet-Exposed Entry Points",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PASTA Stage I defines business and security objectives and maps them to threat actors. An architecture with internet-exposed entry points but no documented threat scenarios means that adversarial thinking has not been applied: the team has not specified who might attack, how, and with what capabilities.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without explicit threat actor profiles and attack scenarios, mitigations are chosen reactively rather than proactively, leaving systematic threat actor capabilities unaddressed.\n\n\nMitigation: Add a threat_scenarios section to the model with at least one scenario per distinct threat actor type (e.g., script-kiddie, insider, nation-state). Each scenario should document entry_assets, kill_chain_steps, and mitigated_by controls.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Does the model have at least one threat_scenarios entry for each internet-facing entry point?\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Threat_Modeling_Cheat_Sheet.html",
              "properties": {
                "cwe": "CWE-693",
                "function": "operations",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "stride": "elevation-of-privilege"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "no-rate-limiting-public-api",
              "name": "Stage V - No Rate Limiting on Internet-Exposed API",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PASTA Stage V identifies weaknesses that enable exploitation. An internet-accessible API endpoint without rate limiting is vulnerable to brute-force credential attacks, credential stuffing, API abuse, and denial-of-service amplification. Rate limiting is a fundamental control for any internet-exposed API.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Brute-force attacks against authentication endpoints, mass data harvesting via enumeration, DDoS amplification, and billing abuse on pay-per-use endpoints.\n\n\nMitigation: Add rate limiting at the API gateway or application layer (token bucket, sliding window). Apply stricter limits on authentication endpoints. Set rate_limited to true on the corresponding communication links after implementation.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Are all internet-exposed API communication links rate-limited?\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Denial_of_Service_Cheat_Sheet.html",
              "properties": {
                "cwe": "CWE-770",
                "function": "operations",
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "stride": "denial-of-service"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "per-operation-rate-limiting-missing",
              "name": "Stage V - Custom-Developed Service Without Per-Operation Rate Limiting",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PASTA Stage V identifies vulnerabilities in the attack surface. Custom-developed services that process authentication credentials or session tokens and have outgoing communication links without rate limiting are vulnerable to credential stuffing, brute-force, bulk data exfiltration, and resource exhaustion. Global API rate limits are too coarse to prevent targeted abuse of individual high-value operations such as login, password reset, or bulk data exports.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Attackers can enumerate users, brute-force credentials, bulk-download sensitive data, or exhaust backend resources by sending unlimited requests to unthrottled operations. Business process integrity degrades when operations can be repeated without throttling.\n\n\nMitigation: Apply rate limiting at the operation level using token-bucket or sliding-window algorithms, enforced per-user for authenticated requests and per-IP for unauthenticated requests. Set rate_limited: true on every communication link once per-operation limits are implemented and verified.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Is rate_limited set to true on every communication link that participates in authentication or sensitive data processing? Are per-user limits enforced in addition to any global API limits?\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Denial_of_Service_Cheat_Sheet.html",
              "properties": {
                "cwe": "CWE-770",
                "function": "operations",
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "stride": "denial-of-service"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "undocumented-entry-point",
              "name": "Stage III - Undocumented Application Entry Point",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PASTA Stage III requires decomposing the application into entry points so that threats can be mapped against known attack surfaces. A technical asset reachable from the internet or marked as internet-exposed that has no entry_point_type declared represents an undocumented entry point that cannot be systematically analysed for threats.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Undocumented entry points are excluded from threat analysis, leaving unknown attack surface that attackers can exploit without detection or mitigation.\n\n\nMitigation: Declare entry_point_type on every internet-exposed technical asset (api, web_ui, cli, file_upload, webhook, cron, or internal_rpc). Maintain an entry point register and review it as part of each release cycle.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Do all internet-exposed technical assets have an entry_point_type declared?"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Attack_Surface_Analysis_Cheat_Sheet.html",
              "properties": {
                "cwe": "CWE-693",
                "function": "architecture",
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "stride": "elevation-of-privilege"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "weak-auth-on-entry-point",
              "name": "Stage VI - Weak Authentication Strength on Internet-Exposed Entry Point",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PASTA Stage VI models how attackers simulate attacks against identified entry points. Internet-exposed entry points with weak authentication (none or password-only) are prime targets for credential stuffing, phishing, and account takeover attacks. High-value or sensitive entry points should require MFA or hardware tokens.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Full account takeover, lateral movement into internal systems, and data exfiltration. Credential stuffing attacks against password-only endpoints succeed at statistically predictable rates given leaked credential databases.\n\n\nMitigation: Require MFA for all user-facing internet entry points handling sensitive data. Declare requires_authentication_strength as mfa or hardware on the technical asset. Consider phishing-resistant authentication (WebAuthn, FIDO2) for high-value assets.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Do all internet-exposed entry points with confidential or higher data have requires_authentication_strength set to mfa or hardware?\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html",
              "properties": {
                "cwe": "CWE-287",
                "function": "architecture",
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "stride": "spoofing"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "internet-reachable-datastore",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Internet-Reachable Datastore: AWS ECR Container Registry can be reached from an internet-exposed asset with no application-tier separation"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "threagile.yaml"
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "threagileSyntheticId": "internet-reachable-datastore@container-registry"
          },
          "suppressions": [
            {
              "kind": "external",
              "justification": "ECR repositories are private (no public registry policy); the asset's internet flag models the managed-service endpoint, not public readability. Pull/push require AWS IAM auth.\n"
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "confidence": 0,
            "dataBreachProbability": "probable",
            "exploitationImpact": "very-high",
            "exploitationLikelihood": "likely",
            "mostRelevantAsset": "container-registry",
            "severity": "elevated",
            "status": "false-positive"
          }
